<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osaka Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> Castle is a fortress in Osaka built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in the late sixteenth century. It was the stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> family after Hideyoshi's death. Its sieges in 1614 and 1615 eliminated the Toyotomi and completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>'s monopoly of political power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osaka-castle.jpg" alt="Osaka Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osaka_Castle_03bs3200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The inner keep of Osaka Castle stands on a plot of roughly one square kilometre in Chūō-ku, Osaka, on two raised platforms of landfill held by sheer walls of cut rock, each overlooking a moat. The keep is five storeys tall outside and eight inside, set on a high stone foundation. An inner moat, wet to the north-east and dry to the south-west, and an outer moat of four water-filled sections surround it. The grounds cover about 61,000 square metres and contain thirteen structures that the Japanese government has designated important cultural assets, among them the Ote-mon and Sakura-mon gates, several turrets and the Kinmeisui Well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> began construction in 1583 on the site of the Ishiyama Hongan-ji, a temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a>. The plan was modelled on Azuchi Castle, the headquarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, which Hideyoshi wanted to match and surpass: a five-storey tower with three more storeys underground and gold leaf on the sides to impress visitors. The inner donjon was complete in 1585 and the whole castle in 1597. Hideyoshi died the next year and the castle passed to his son, Toyotomi Hideyori.</p><p>In the winter of 1614 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> besieged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> forces. Though outnumbered about two to one, they held off an army of 200,000 and protected the outer walls, but Ieyasu had the outer moat filled in. When Hideyori began to restore it in summer 1615, Ieyasu's armies returned and routed the defenders on 4 June. Hideyori and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a> killed themselves, the buildings burned, and the Toyotomi line ended.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> then took direct control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1619 and rebuilt the castle. The granite walls raised in the 1620s still stand, laid without mortar, and many stones carry the crests of the clans that supplied them. A new keep, begun in 1628, was built in a different part of the main bailey, over the buried base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> tower.</p><p>Lightning ignited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> store in 1660, and in 1665 it burned the keep down. In 1868 the castle surrendered to anti-bakufu imperial loyalists, and many buildings burned in the conflicts around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. Under the new government the site became the Osaka Army Arsenal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A ferroconcrete keep was built in 1931. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the arsenal employed 60,000 workers, and on 14 August 1945 American bombing destroyed 90 per cent of it and killed 382 people. A restoration approved in 1995 and finished in 1997 made the keep a concrete reproduction of the original tower, with a museum inside. The castle is open to the public and is crowded during the cherry blossom season.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osaka Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The destruction of the Toyotomi house in the sieges of <b>Osaka Castle</b> in 1614 and 1615 removed the last plausible rival source of legitimacy and completed the Tokugawa monopoly of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As Tokugawa forces took <b>Osaka Castle</b>, Toyotomi Hideyori and his mother took their own lives.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Hideyoshi built <b>Osaka Castle</b> on the site, and the Hongan-ji later split into two branches.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishiyama-hongan-ji">Ishiyama Hongan-ji</a></li></ul></section>
